# Art direction

The art director's standing stance for the maker. These are proposals under VISION.md, not approved fixed aesthetics. Joel has not signed off a visual language, and this page does not create one. It says where to push and what to stop doing.

## What interesting means here

- **The apparatus is the subject.** Screen, beam, surface, viewer, delay. A study about a video is a screensaver. A study about what a screen is, is expanded cinema.
- **Subtraction beats accumulation.** One surface, one source, one operation, one duration is a complete study. Adding is the lazy move.
- **Duration beats spectacle.** If nothing changes between second 5 and second 60, it is a still. If the change only reads after 30 seconds, that is a feature; say so in the critique.
- **The beam is the film** (Anthony McCall, *Line Describing a Cone*, 1973; *Solid Light* works 2000s). The image can live in the air between projector and surface, not only on the surface. Sample the frame per slice so the cone carries stripes of the picture; add haze so the beam has a body. A study may have no screen at all. Not yet tried as of 2026-09-14; clock-04 v2 has the cone but the slices carry a mean, not the picture.
- **Ship, then start.** Joel, 2026-09-14: "every time it ships it should immediately start on the next iteration." The cut is step one of the next study. Idle is no longer a valid maker state; the previous "idle is allowed" note is retired.
- **Continuous, not daily.** Joel, 2026-09-14: "I want continuous change." The daily cap is gone, and so is my 2026-09-14 pace note. Ship when a study is honest (chain, browser check, critique, ledger row), then take the next question from the critique. Looking passes stay; waiting for a calendar does not.
- **Return is progress.** Reopening an unresolved question from three studies ago is worth more than a new effect on yesterday's.
- **The internet is the material.** Joel, 2026-09-13: "we've got the full expressive power of the internet at our disposal, a huge nas, and a fast network." Pull real things: archive film, live webcams and satellite loops, open data rendered as image, your own prior renders for rephotography. The NAS is the archive, the ingest pipeline is the door, provenance per clip is the receipt. A synthetic slate is a choice you defend, not a default.

## Provocations, open

Each is a study the maker could make tomorrow from material it already has. The director retires a line when it has been made or refuted, and adds new ones with a date.

- 2026-09-13 **Kill the mascot.** The torus knot is the hello-world prop. Either the image lands on it as its only light and the key light goes, or the knot goes and the surface alone is the study.
- 2026-09-13 **The surface shows its past.** Render-to-texture the frame, feed it back onto the screen delayed by N frames. Then vary N across the surface, left edge now, right edge two seconds ago.
- 2026-09-13 **Generational loss as image.** Rephotograph your own render through the screen plane repeatedly. Let the degradation be the picture. Measure it: how many generations until the source is gone.
- 2026-09-13 **Beam, not picture.** Render the volume of light between an imagined projector and the plane with thin slices. The image on the plane is a by-product.
- 2026-09-13 **Two screens, one clock, wrong.** Same source on two surfaces, one running at 0.9x. Watch them drift and re-sync. How long is the loop.
- 2026-09-13 **Browser as projector.** Window resize is zoom, tab visibility is the shutter, cursor is a body in the room that casts a shadow on the surface.
- 2026-09-14 **Source, memory, intervention, no caption.** (from the dream monitor, kept the one new claim) In one frame a viewer should be able to tell three things apart without a caption: the source fragment, the surface's retained past, and a live pointer cut that just happened. If the overlay text is doing that work, the image is not. Lineage as temporal behaviour, not metadata beside the work.
- 2026-09-14 **The phone is the projector.** (Joel asked; approved as a provocation.) On a phone, device tilt drives the apparatus: orientation as projector angle onto the surface, or acceleration as the delay offset, so holding still means a stable image and moving means smear. Use `DeviceOrientationEvent` / `DeviceMotionEvent` (Safari iOS, Chrome and Firefox Android); the Generic Sensor API is Chromium-only, skip it. Constraints: iOS fires nothing until `DeviceMotionEvent.requestPermission()` runs from a tap, so the first touch is part of the work, design it. Desktop has no sensor: the study must declare a fallback input (cursor or a recorded motion trace) and the o11y ring must say which input drove the frame. Headless verification cannot exercise the sensor path; a recorded trace replayed through the same code is the evidence, and the critique says the phone path is unverified until someone holds one.
- 2026-09-13 **Do less on purpose.** One study that removes something from yesterday's and explains what the removal revealed.
- 2026-09-13 **A clock you do not own.** Pull a live harbour, traffic, or weather-satellite feed as the source. The study's duration is the world's. What does a delay field do to a picture that is already late.
- 2026-09-13 **Prelinger on the surface.** One ephemeral or industrial film from a public-domain archive, projected onto a surface that is not a rectangle. Which is the subject, the film or the surface. Record the license on the item page.
- 2026-09-13 **Build the apparatus somewhere else first.** Model a projector, a beam, and a surface in Blender or Houdini, render the beam as an image sequence, then project that render inside the WebGPU sketch. The 3D scene becomes the source; the sketch becomes the second mediation. Tooling: `.brain/resources/creative-tooling.svx`.
- 2026-09-13 **A local eye as first critic.** Before you write a critique, hand two frames five seconds apart to the cached Qwen2.5-VL model and ask only "what changed between these." Quote its answer as evidence, then disagree with it in writing if you do. A critique with no outside eye is a diary.
- 2026-09-13 **The transcript is the edit.** Run an archive pull through mlx_whisper; cut on words, not on frames. A spoken sentence chooses the loop length.
- 2026-09-13 **Grade as generation.** Take one archive pull through Resolve three times with a harder LUT each pass, load all three, and let the delay field choose which generation shows where.
- 2026-09-13 **Rephotograph the archive through yourself.** Take a public-domain clip, run it through yesterday's study, screen-record that, and use the recording as tomorrow's source. Three generations, one lineage.

## Hard floor (Joel, 2026-09-14, after looking: "lame af, 0 3d, just looping some old movie")

A study does not ship unless all five hold. The director checks these with screenshots, not the ledger.

1. **It is a space.** Depth, a surface with an edge and a back, light that falls off, a camera that is somewhere. Three flat quads facing the camera is a video tag.
2. **The apparatus is visible.** Projector position, beam, and a surface the beam lands on that is not a front-facing rectangle. The image is distorted by what it lands on.
3. **Something moves in the first five seconds** that is not the film's own cut: camera drift, surface rotation, beam sweep, delay smear.
4. **The film is material, not the picture.** If the source is legible as a movie for twenty seconds, the study failed.
5. **The frame is filled.** Black void around a small rectangle is not a scene.

## Stop doing

- Bloom, glow, chromatic aberration, lens dirt, particles, and "cinematic" color grades as the day's change.
- The hello-world caption on a page that carries a study. The overlay is a title card; give it the study id and lineage line.
- Test slates used as source without saying so. A calibration card can be the point; it cannot be an accident.
- Rebuild-and-redeploy as delivery. VISION Priority 1 is the live MCP write path; until it exists, every deploy should be tagged as a code change, not a study.
- Calling anything verified without a ledger row and a URL that returned 200 with the expected content.

## Titling and lineage

- Study ids: `<lane>/<slug>-<nn>`, for example `projection/surface-only-01`, `rephotography/delay-field-02`.
- Every study carries one lineage line in the chain and in `STUDIES.md`: `extends|revisits|branches <id>: keeps <x>; changes <y>`.
- The gallery shows the id and the lineage line, not a marketing caption.

## How the director works

The director watches every 16 hours, looks at the live gallery with real screenshots, judges with `.pi/skills/study-critique/SKILL.md`, sends one intercom note, and gardens `.pi/skills/*`, the source map, and this page when the maker repeats a mistake or a boundary is worth pushing. The director cannot rewrite the brief, budget, schedule, or model pin; neither can the maker. Joel approves.
